# Forge — per-machine engine settings.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/forge/forge.env and edit. That path is per-machine on
# purpose: the same vault checkout is a GPU box on one machine and a laptop
# borrowing a hosted endpoint on another, and the vault is shared by Git.
#
#     mkdir -p ~/.config/forge
#     cp config/forge.env.example ~/.config/forge/forge.env
#     chmod 600 ~/.config/forge/forge.env      # it can hold an API key
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. `#` comments, blank lines, a leading
# `export `, and surrounding quotes are all fine. No interpolation, no command
# substitution — a settings file that can execute is one that can surprise you.
#
# The process environment always wins, so any line here can be overridden for a
# single command:  FORGE_LLM_PROVIDER=mock forge status
#
# Check what got loaded with:  forge status
# Full reference:              docs/cli.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile A — the GPU box (Ollama), with a hosted fallback.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORGE_LLM_FALLBACK is opt-in and off by default. When set, Forge health-checks
# Ollama ONCE before any work; if it is down, the whole run uses the fallback
# instead. It never switches mid-run — the model identity is part of every
# derivation key, so a per-call swap would attribute results to a model that
# did not produce them. `forge status` shows which one is actually serving.

FORGE_MODEL_DEFAULT=qwen3:8b
FORGE_LLM_TIMEOUT=420

# FORGE_LLM_FALLBACK=cloud
# FORGE_CLOUD_PRESET=groq
# FORGE_CLOUD_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile B — a laptop with no GPU, running everything on a hosted endpoint.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No paid API is required anywhere in Forge. FORGE_CLOUD_VENDOR=openai is a
# *wire format*, not a company — every host below speaks it.
#
# A preset fills in the endpoint, the credential variable name, and a sane
# token ceiling. You still choose the model: that is never guessed for you,
# because free model line-ups rotate and a stale default fails confusingly.
# Check the provider's model list, then confirm with `forge model-test`.
#
# FORGE_LLM_PROVIDER=cloud
# FORGE_CLOUD_PRESET=groq
# FORGE_CLOUD_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
# FORGE_LLM_TIMEOUT=420

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile C — a laptop driving the GPU box over a private network.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use a Tailscale/WireGuard hostname rather than a public address — Ollama has
# no authentication, so anything that can reach port 11434 can use it.
#
# FORGE_OLLAMA_URL=http://asus:11434
# FORGE_MODEL_DEFAULT=qwen3:8b
# FORGE_LLM_TIMEOUT=420

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosted presets — endpoints verified 2026-08-20, model ids re-checked
# 2026-08-29. Treat this as a typo-guard, not an integration: endpoints change
# and **model ids rotate faster than this file does**. The explicit variables
# always win. `forge status` now verifies the configured model against the
# host's own list, so a stale id here is caught before a run rather than after.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# groq        https://api.groq.com/openai        GROQ_API_KEY
#             Free: no card. Fast (LPU). Per-model daily caps.
#             VERIFY THE MODEL ID BEFORE A RUN. Groq decommissions models, and
#             a retired id fails every call while `forge status` used to say
#             OK — `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` was named here and was gone by
#             2026-08-29. `curl -s https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/models
#             -H "Authorization: Bearer $GROQ_API_KEY"` lists what is live;
#             pick one whose supported_features include structured_outputs.
# cerebras    https://api.cerebras.ai            CEREBRAS_API_KEY
#             Free: no card, ~1M tokens/day. Context capped at 8K on free,
#             which is why this preset sets max_tokens=4096 rather than 8192.
# openrouter  https://openrouter.ai/api          OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#             Free model ids end in `:free`. Only ~50 requests/day without
#             credits — fine for `forge model-test`, NOT for an extraction run.
# together    https://api.together.xyz           TOGETHER_API_KEY
# fireworks   https://api.fireworks.ai/inference FIREWORKS_API_KEY
#
# Local OpenAI-compatible servers (they ignore the credential, but one must be
# present, so these name a variable you can set to anything):
# lmstudio    http://localhost:1234    llama-cpp  http://localhost:8080
# vllm        http://localhost:8000
#
# Any other OpenAI-compatible host works with no preset:
# FORGE_CLOUD_VENDOR=openai and FORGE_CLOUD_BASE_URL=https://...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * The key is read at call time and never written to config, the derived
#   store, provenance, or logs — and never copied into the environment.
#   `forge status` reports whether one is present without echoing it.
# * Open-weights models cap output well below a frontier model. A preset picks
#   a safe ceiling; override with FORGE_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS if the host rejects it.
# * A measured quality result belongs to a *model*, not to Forge. Changing
#   provider or model means re-running the evaluation, not inheriting the old
#   number — see docs/cli.md, "Re-measure after any provider change".
# * Model identity is part of the derivation key, so switching provider or
#   model invalidates cached extractions by design. That is what keeps results
#   from two models from being mixed — not an inefficiency to work around.
